# What the install job installs to run the tests on the Pythons that are past their end of life
# (ci.yml): 3.9 and 3.10 for now.
#
# This is a COMPLETE lock. Compiled with the oldest Python the package supports (3.9), it holds
# the right version of every package for every Python from 3.9 up, 3.11 and 3.12 included, with
# markers that say which applies where.
# Which Pythons use it is decided by the matrix in ci.yml, not by this file. So retiring a Python
# to it never changes this lock, only ci.yml and the --python-version of the live lock in
# update.sh. And a lock must never be copied into this folder: the live one only covers the
# Pythons it was compiled for, and the entries of the older ones would be lost.
#
# Dependabot cannot maintain a lock like this one: it takes one of the versions of a package as
# "the current one", then checks that the newest can be pinned for every Python the lock covers,
# which cannot be when the newest release of a tool needs a newer Python than the oldest one, and
# reports an error. So this folder is not one of its own (see dependabot.yml), and it is next to
# .github/requirements/ rather than inside it: Dependabot also fetches the subfolders of the
# folder it is given.
# `bash .github/requirements/update.sh --upgrade` refreshes it when that is worth it: uv gives
# each Python the newest version it supports.
#
# pytest-cov and setuptools are for what comes with the tests of the package: the coverage report,
# and the tests of the tools that build it (src/_build_utils.py), which import setuptools and
# distutils, which a Python from 3.12 on does not come with.
pytest
pytest-cov
setuptools
